        <p class="lead text-xl text-gray-700 font-medium">
          An assessor knows within minutes whether they will read your funding application to the end. In this third episode of PerfEco Institutions, we walk round to the other side of the desk: what they open first, and what they conclude from it.
        </p>

        <h2>On the other side of the desk</h2>

        <p>
          I spent twenty years inside European and international institutions, on the side of the people who read the applications. That job has its habits, and project teams rarely get to see them. Here is how it works from the assessor's chair.
        </p>

        <p>
          The starting point is simple. An assessor has a stack of applications in front of them and limited time for each one.
        </p>

        <h2>Three habits that work against the application</h2>

        <p>
          To give themselves every chance, project teams often do three things. All three come from a good place.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>They add appendices.</strong> Studies, letters of support, brochures: the aim is to show how much work has gone in. The file gets thicker, and the essentials get lost between the attachments.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>They reuse the wording of the call.</strong> Every criterion is quoted, every priority ticked. The project ends up sounding like every other project written in response to the same call.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>They do the budget last.</strong> The narrative is finally ready, the deadline is close, and the financial table gets filled in. The narrative and the budget then describe two slightly different projects.
        </p>

        <h2>What the assessor opens first</h2>

        <p>
          Before reading the narrative from start to finish, they open three pages.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>The first page.</strong> They look for the project in one sentence: who is behind it, who it is for, and how much it costs. If they have to piece that sentence together themselves, the reading starts on the wrong foot.</li>
          <li><strong>The budget.</strong> They set it against the narrative. Every activity described should have a cost line, and every cost line should point back to an activity.</li>
          <li><strong>The timeline.</strong> They check that the team on paper can actually meet those dates. Twelve activities over eighteen months, carried by one person working half-time, is obvious at a glance.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What gets an application set aside in two minutes</h2>

        <p>
          Four signs are enough to stop the reading, however good the project is.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>A mandatory document is missing.</strong> The rules of the call are explicit, and the assessor has to apply them to the letter.</li>
          <li><strong>The budget tells a different story.</strong> An activity with no cost, or a cost with no activity: the assessor starts wondering which of the two documents is telling the truth.</li>
          <li><strong>A figure changes from one page to the next.</strong> Page 2 and page 14 give different totals. It is often just an old version left in, and it reads as a lack of rigour.</li>
          <li><strong>The objectives cannot be measured.</strong> "Improve", "strengthen", "develop", with no date and no figure: at the end, nobody will be able to say whether the project worked.</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-teal rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-1">A diagnostic question</p>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">After one minute, can an outside reader sum up your project in one sentence? Does every line of your budget point to an activity described in the narrative? And does the application say who does what, and with how much time?</p>
        </div>

        <h2>What the committee never puts in writing</h2>

        <p>
          One question hangs over every application the committee discusses: "Will this team see it through?" It appears on no scoring grid and in no rejection letter. Yet it weighs on the decision.
        </p>

        <p>
          The answer shows in three details. A realistic timeline. Names next to tasks. A budget that pays for the time of the people who will run the project. A lean, consistent application reassures the committee.
        </p>

        <h2>Reading your own application like an assessor</h2>

        <p>
          Three read-throughs are enough to see your application through the eyes of the person who will receive it.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>The first page.</strong> Write the project in one sentence at the top of the page: who is behind it, who it is for, how much. If that sentence runs past three lines, the project is not sharp enough yet.</li>
          <li><strong>The cross-checked budget.</strong> Put the narrative on the left and the budget on the right. For each activity, find its cost line. For each cost line, find its activity. Check that every figure quoted is the same everywhere.</li>
          <li><strong>The team.</strong> Next to each major task in the timeline, write a name and the time that person will spend on it.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The five-minute test</h2>

        <p>
          Our advice: before you submit, give it to someone who does not know the project. Give them five minutes, no more. Then ask them what they understood.
        </p>

        <p>
          If they can sum up your project in one sentence, your first page is doing its job. If they hesitate, you know which page to rework — and you know it before the assessor does.
        </p>

